## The Cold, Hard Numbers: A Year of Food Recalls

As of this reporting, 2026 has already witnessed an alarming 767 food recalls. To contextualize this, the total number of recalls documented historically stands at 1125. This means that in just seven months, nearly 68% of all documented recalls have occurred this year alone. Noteworthy are the 209 Class I recalls, indicating significant health risks.

## Repeat Offenders: The Usual Suspects

A sobering 94 brands have been identified as repeat offenders this year. Among the worst, Spring & Mulberry Inc. and Moonlight Packing Corporation are tied with an egregious 96 rating due to their 11 Class I recalls each. Other notable mentions include Ghirardelli Chocolate Company and Everpress Juice.

## Recent Highlights (or Lowlights?)

The most recent recalls include:

- **Wells Enterprises**' French Vanilla due to elevated coliform levels.
- **MOGO Moringa** capsules, contaminated with **Salmonella Typhimurium**.
- **Western Mixers Produce & Nuts**' dark chocolate raisins, containing undeclared peanuts.

## Analysis: A Worsening Trend?

With 212 recalls recorded in June alone, 2026 seems to be on track to outpace previous years. Recalls due to allergens, Salmonella, and foreign objects lead the charge, showing the need for better quality control practices in the food industry.

## Conclusion

This year has been a wake-up call for both manufacturers and consumers. With over 700 recalls already this year, it is clear that vigilance is not optional. Consumers are urged to stay informed and regularly check for recall notices.

Stay safe, and always read the fine print.
